<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A library is written in Kotlin and works perfectly in the Android app. Adding iOS fails because the artifact is JVM-only. The language on the repository badge did not describe the targets your build could consume.</p>

Evaluate the exact library version, exported API and behaviour before choosing a replacement.

<!-- @after A Staff-Level Dependency Evaluation :: visual -->
### Four pieces of portability evidence

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node"><strong>Artifact</strong><small>A compatible variant exists for every required target</small></div><div class="node"><strong>API</strong><small>The common surface avoids unsupported platform types</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Behaviour</strong><small>Cancellation, storage and locale semantics fit the product</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Operation</strong><small>Builds, diagnostics, maintenance and exit cost are acceptable</small></div></div><figcaption>A library may use Java internally in its JVM variant while offering a common API and separate native implementation.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Future-Target Failure Modes :: animation -->
### Add a new target without hiding a missing feature

<figure class="stepper"><span class="stepper-title">Add a new target without hiding a missing feature</span><div class="stage viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node" data-el="n0"><strong>Existing targets</strong><small data-text="v0">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="n1"><strong>New target</strong><small data-text="v1">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="n2"><strong>Dependency seam</strong><small data-text="v2">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="n3"><strong>Product decision</strong><small data-text="v3">—</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[{"label": "Expand", "caption": "Android and iOS work. A proposed web target changes the required support matrix, so repeat the dependency evaluation.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "active", "n1": "idle", "n2": "idle", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Existing evidence", "v1": "New requirement", "v2": "Audit exact versions", "v3": "No portability assumption"}}, {"label": "Resolve", "caption": "One dependency has no compatible variant for the new target. Capture the failing configuration and published metadata.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "active", "n2": "idle", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Still supported", "v1": "Resolution fails", "v2": "Unsupported implementation", "v3": "Visible limitation"}}, {"label": "Choose", "caption": "Consider a supported version, replacement, narrower source set or an explicit platform adapter.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "idle", "n2": "active", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Preserve working paths", "v1": "Needs real support", "v2": "Replaceable boundary", "v3": "Costed alternatives"}}, {"label": "Validate", "caption": "Build the new consumer and test its behaviour. A silent no-op is not a valid substitute for a required security or persistence capability.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "idle", "n2": "idle", "n3": "active"}, "text": {"v0": "Regression checks", "v1": "Target evidence", "v2": "Contract preserved", "v3": "Accept or defer target"}}]</script><figcaption>Illustrative sequence, not a measured trace. <a href="https://kotlinlang.org/docs/api-guidelines-build-for-multiplatform.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Primary contract</a>.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Platform Escape Hatches :: check -->
### Try the decision yourself

<details><summary>Does Kotlin source imply commonMain support?</summary><p>No. Artifact variants, dependencies and API availability decide compatibility.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can a common dependency use Java in its Android implementation?</summary><p>Yes, if the common API and other target implementations are valid for their compilations.</p></details>
<details><summary>When is a no-op adapter unacceptable?</summary><p>When omitting the required behaviour silently breaks correctness, security or the product contract.</p></details>

